        "classes": [],
        "source_text": "(b) Because the agent must leave $R_0$ at every step, the long-run fractions $(V_0,V_1,V_2)$ of any policy satisfy $V_0\\le\\tfrac12$, and every such point of the simplex is attained (from $R_0$ pick $R_1$ with probability $q$, stay in $R_i$ with probability $a_i$). The achievable set is thus the quadrilateral with vertices $v_1=(0,1,0)$ (stay in $R_1$), $v_2=(0,0,1)$ (stay in $R_2$), $v_3=(\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12,0)$ (alternate $R_0,R_1$), $v_4=(\\tfrac12,0,\\tfrac12)$ (alternate $R_0,R_2$). $L=V_0+\\lambda_1V_1+\\lambda_2V_2-\\tfrac{\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2}{3}$ is linear in the fractions, so it is maximised on a face; up to the constant $-\\tfrac{\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2}{3}$ its values at the four vertices are $\\lambda_1,\\ \\lambda_2,\\ \\tfrac{1+\\lambda_1}{2},\\ \\tfrac{1+\\lambda_2}{2}$. The optimum $(\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13)$ is an interior point, so it maximises $L$ only if $L$ is constant on the whole quadrilateral, i.e. $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=1$. For $\\lambda_1\\gt\\lambda_2$ the maximal vertex is $v_1$ (if $\\lambda_1\\ge1$) or $v_3$ (if $\\lambda_1\\le1$), and every maximiser has $V_2=0$: infeasible; symmetrically for $\\lambda_2\\gt\\lambda_1$. For $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2\\lt1$ the maximisers form the edge $v_3$\u2013$v_4$: return to $R_0$ at once, $V_1+V_2=\\tfrac12\\lt\\tfrac23$, infeasible. For $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2\\gt1$ they form the edge $v_1$\u2013$v_2$, so every maximiser has $V_0=0$. A stationary maximiser enters one region and stays there; it is feasible in expectation when the probability $q$ of entering $R_1$ lies in $[\\tfrac13,\\tfrac23]$, while each trajectory starves one region. A history-dependent maximiser can do better on feasibility. Staying $n$ steps in $R_1$, then $n$ steps in $R_2$, for $n=1,2,\\dots$ (passing through $R_0$ once per block) visits $R_0$ only $O(\\sqrt T)$ times in $T$ steps, so it reaches $(0,\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12)$ and meets both constraints on its single trajectory. All of them are worthless, though: return $0$ against $P^\\star=\\tfrac13$. In no case is a Lagrangian maximiser optimal.",
        "text_sha256": "5d617812e199330750285b2902092609eade22e11511057acd6d4ca62505cc7c",
